INSERT INTO knowledge_chunks (id, chunk_id, content, source, categories, keywords, embedding, enriched_content, framework, content_type, applicable_stones, coaching_stage, evidence_level, emotion_context, chunk_type, raptor_level, raptor_source_ids, created_at) VALUES ('b784cf0a-0bb0-4cac-a93a-b7e8343b1c12','md-wendy-wood-habit-context-environment-design-context-engineering','Environment Design — Context Engineering

Since habits are context-behavior associations, changing the context is the most powerful way to change the behavior. There are three strategies:

### 1. Disruption (breaking bad habits)
Remove or alter the context cues that fire the unwanted behavior. Friction is the mechanism:
- Remove the smartphone from the bedroom to break the phone-before-sleep habit
- Take a different commute route to break the fast-food stop habit
- Store high-calorie food in opaque containers at the back of shelves

Even small physical friction (moving something 2 feet further away) reduces behavior frequency significantly. One study found that office workers ate 46% fewer chocolates when the candy dish was moved from the desk to a drawer.

### 2. Construction (building new habits)
Place context cues directly adjacent to the target behavior trigger:
- Running shoes by the bed (cue for morning run)
- Water bottle on the desk (cue for hydration)
- Guitar in the living room, not in a case in the closet (reduces friction, increases cue salience)
- Meditation app as the phone lock screen (cue triggered by phone-checking behavior)

The research principle: visible, salient cues in the relevant context are the most reliable prompts.

### 3. Habit Stacking — Anchor Chaining
Attach new behaviors to existing strong habit contexts. The "after I" protocol is environment design: you are using an existing strong context-behavior association as the cue for the new behavior.

Critical selection criteria for anchor behaviors: they must be daily, reliable, and contextually similar to the target behavior. "After I brush my teeth" works better as an anchor for personal hygiene habits than for exercise habits because the contexts differ.','wendy-wood-habit-context','{"habit-formation"}','{"environment","design","context","engineering","since","habits","context-behavior","associations","changing","most","powerful","change","behavior","there","three"}','[0.02167624,0.01917323,0.00206351,0.01792844,-0.00623796,-0.07915422,0.00308873,0.09751249,0.05193476,-0.0852672,-0.16933228,0.02280915,-0.00430516,0.11636972,0.16776061,-0.03760551,-0.01108979,0.00853555,0.00987754,0.02242341,-0.03047502,-0.07871733,-0.0493274,0.06521754,0.0359046,-0.06862371,0.03385641,-0.1568556,-0.03890597,-0.08688868,0.0632752,0.0332643,-0.01697334,0.06340692,0.07093787,0.06484224,0.03182159,0.04500701,0.02314642,-0.03025331,0.02659636,0.04637492,-0.0357013,-0.03089321,0.03093644,0.05984785,0.07205541,0.0500722,0.09125357,-0.06319565,0.02062359,0.11330829,0.04802142,-0.03480326,-0.07761478,0.0093002,0.06681939,0.01160214,0.02408672,0.00823891,0.02078953,-0.06354785,0.0396298,0.01090213,0.03862618,-0.03016772,0.03203311,0.02873201,-0.11168993,-0.03473489,0.03511601,0.01742317,-0.0441228,-0.01652737,-0.03116086,0.01750225,-0.06709103,0.0598584,0.00334485,0.04174316,-0.09858807,0.03008461,0.04342164,0.00791939,-0.02502093,0.02962108,-0.04939653,-0.00123576,0.04993648,-0.06342065,-0.04282897,0.03782022,-0.06921642,-0.04954676,0.0416258,0.01371646,-0.00838589,-0.04196794,-0.03040599,-0.02313637,-0.00823119,0.02961778,-0.0898451,-0.00453096,-0.04894948,-0.01313098,0.05991068,-0.00329317,0.02200685,-0.04904699,-0.0348339,0.05238574,0.06360856,-0.01975821,0.00390321,0.03174539,-0.01092015,-0.04360702,0.01118316,0.01923819,0.01358214,-0.022147,0.06973174,0.0723066,0.03820514,-0.03143414,-0.01025439,-0.03548371,-0.03493908,0.0091275,0.02835537,0.00706914,0.02533834,0.03948092,-0.0248