98,0.00235797,-0.02193934,-0.00621146,-0.02475802,-0.00949898,-0.00412629,-0.01866831,-0.00255661,0.00142339,0.00819692,0.00566616,0.00501366,-0.04119969,-0.0338995,-0.00908119,0.03377285,-0.01502713,0.02091929,-0.04877544,0.02894838,-0.0150482,0.01876638,-0.01795278,0.00462749,0.01271483,-0.02607387,-0.00210533,-0.01194539,0.01824453,-0.02741723,0.01243908,0.02984309,0.00810944,-0.01311309,0.02366502,-0.02559802,0.02515824,0.01516117,0.01974813,-0.01076169,0.01558223,0.03444375,0.00437405,0.02604897,-0.01118635,0.01565797,-0.00925685,0.01238118,-0.01850091,0.00456157,0.01708891,0.01589581,0.0062292,-0.01792188,0.01875262,-0.01876081,-0.03411642,-0.01921475,-0.00957473,-0.02725039,0.00505041,0.01776575,-0.02996656,0.03378526,-0.00653717,0.00467372,0.00341722,0.03719267,-0.00664808,-0.01404198,-0.00445071,0.01274526,-0.01141264,0.03901573,-0.02515268,-0.01093089,-0.01193616,0.00603606]'::vector,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{}',NULL,NULL,'{}','raptor_summary',1,'{"md-mindset-and-grit-growth-mindset-carol-dweck","md-mindset-and-grit-praising-process-not-ability","md-mindset-and-grit-grit-angela-duckworth","md-mindset-and-grit-the-plateau-of-latent-potential","md-mindset-and-grit-struggling-and-self-compassion"}','2026-04-02T06:01:52.797047+00:00') ON CONFLICT (chunk_id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO knowledge_chunks (id, chunk_id, content, source, categories, keywords, embedding, enriched_content, framework, content_type, applicable_stones, coaching_stage, evidence_level, emotion_context, chunk_type, raptor_level, raptor_source_ids, created_at) VALUES ('9f1d68ea-8ae8-4399-b925-9bcd7273be20','raptor_l1_motivational_interviewing_c1','Motivational Interviewing (MI) is a goal-oriented communication style that strengthens a person''s motivation for and commitment to change by eliciting and reinforcing their own arguments for change. Developed by William Miller and Stephen Rollnick, MI is based on the insight that ambivalence is normal and expected at the onset of any change attempt. The core principles of MI include partnership, acceptance, compassion, and evocation, which guide the use of four core skills: open questions, affirmations, reflective listening, and summaries. By eliciting change talk and reinforcing it with reflections and affirmations, while avoiding argumentation against sustain talk, MI practitioners can increase the likelihood of successful behavior change. The most actionable finding for habit coaching is to use the OARS skills in a funnel structure to assess a person''s readiness for change and tailor the conversation to their specific needs and motivations.','motivational-interviewing (summary)','{"habit-formation"}','{"what","motivational","interviewing","collaborative","goal-oriented","communication","style","designed","strengthen","person''s","motivation","commitment","change","works","eliciting","spirit","four","core","principles","partnership","coach","person","work","together","equals","expert","life","acceptance","absolute","worth","oars","skills","open","questions","cannot","answered","invite","reflection","personal","elaboration","walk","through","talk","sustain","statements","favor","categories","darn-cat","desire","want","ability","reasons","help","need","readiness","ruler","primary","drivers","scale","0-10","important","m